      "subtitle": "Auto-sensing acupressure, no needles",
      "material": "ABS and aluminium alloy",
      "descriptionHtml": "<p><b>An acupressure pen you can actually check the specs on.</b> Most listings in this category tell you what the device will do for your body. This one tells you what it is: a 158 g pen in an ABS and aluminium-alloy body, nine intensity levels on a lit display, five screw-on heads, and a single AA cell. Press the tip to a point, pick a level, and that is the whole machine.</p>\n\n<h3>What you actually get</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<b>Five heads, oil-free</b> — rounded ball, ridged, fine point and flat spoon, plus the fitted tip on the pen. Every head is in the box. None is held back as a paid extra.</li>\n<li>\n<b>Nine levels, held on the display</b> — the level you set stays on screen until you change it, so the setting that suited you yesterday is the one you return to, not a guess at the same number of button presses.</li>\n<li>\n<b>Auto point-sensing tip</b> — the tip responds as it passes over an active point, so you do not need a meridian chart memorised before you start.</li>\n<li>\n<b>One AA cell</b> — no charger, no cable, no charging cradle, and no battery that dies at three years old and takes the device with it. It travels in hand luggage without an argument.</li>\n<li>\n<b>170 mm, 158 g</b> — long enough to reach between your own shoulder blades one-handed, light enough to hold there.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Honest specifications</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Body: ABS and aluminium alloy</li>\n<li>Weight: 158 g, oil-free build, without packaging</li>\n<li>Heads: five, interchangeable, threaded (not friction-fit)</li>\n<li>Intensity: nine selectable levels</li>\n<li>Power: one AA cell, not included</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>What it is, and what it is not</h3>\n<p>This is a consumer wellness and massage tool that applies pressure through a metal tip along with a low-level electrical pulse, in the same broad family as the TENS units sold in any pharmacy. It is <b>not a medical device</b>. It is not cleared by the FDA or Health Canada, and nothing here is a health claim or a treatment for any condition. If a listing for one of these tells you it cures something, that is the moment to close the tab.</p>\n\n<p>Do not use it if you have a pacemaker or any implanted electronic device, or if you are pregnant. Avoid broken skin, the front and sides of the neck, and the area near the eyes. If you have a heart condition, epilepsy or reduced sensation, ask a doctor first.</p>\n\n<h3>Common questions</h3>\n<p><b>Do I need to know the pressure points first?</b><br>No. The auto-sensing tip responds as it passes over an active point, which is what most people use to find where to work.</p>\n<p><b>How long should a session be?</b><br>Five to fifteen minutes across a few points, once or twice a day. Longer is not better. If a spot goes numb, sore or red, stop and move on.</p>\n<p><b>What is the difference between the heads?</b><br>The fine point concentrates on one spot, the ball and spoon spread the same pressure over a wider area, and the ridged head suits a broad muscle like a calf or a forearm.</p>\n<p><b>Is a battery included?</b><br>No. It takes one standard AA cell, which keeps the pen cheap to run and legal to fly with.</p>\n",
